.TH DBMERGE 1 "July 2026" "sumtag 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dbmerge \- combine per-volume sumtag databases into one
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dbmerge
.BI \-\-database " TARGET"
.RB [ \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-progress ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-mixed ]
.IR SOURCE ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dbmerge
folds any number of per-volume
.BR sumtag (1)
databases into one
.IR TARGET ,
so that cross-volume
.BR dedupe (1)
and
.BR grouper (1)
runs have the single database they consume. The sumtag schema was always
multi-volume (the mountpoints table exists so one database can describe
files across many filesystems); one database per volume is an operational
choice that buys parallel scanning, SQLite being a single-writer store.
.B dbmerge
closes the loop: the per-volume databases stay the scanning targets, and
the merged database is a rebuildable analysis artifact, re-merged whenever
the sources move on (after a rescan, or after
.B sumtag \-\-prune\-all
reconciles a source).
.PP
Each
.I SOURCE
is opened read-only, always, and is never modified. It is an experimental
companion to
.BR sumtag (1)
and never computes a checksum: only the mountpoints and files tables flow,
always from source to target.
.SH REPLACE PER MOUNTPOINT
For each mountpoint present in a source, the target's existing rows for
that mountpoint are deleted and the source's rows inserted fresh. A
row-level upsert would carry updates over but never remove target rows
whose source rows were pruned \(em the merged database would accumulate
ghost rows forever. Replacement makes the merge idempotent and each source
authoritative for its own mountpoints, including an empty one: a source
mountpoint with zero rows still clears the target's rows for it. Target
mountpoints no source mentions are left untouched, so one volume can be
re-merged without feeding all of them.
.PP
The corollary is the collision refusal: the same mountpoint path recorded
in two sources is an error, because the second source's replacement would
delete the first's just-merged rows. Sources must partition by mountpoint
\(em exactly what one database per volume produces. This doubles as the
guard against silently interleaving two different filesystems recorded
under one mount path.
.SH WHAT IS NEVER COPIED
.BR grouper (1)'s
derived tables (dirs, dir_files, dir_pairs, groups, group_dirs,
grouper_meta) are never copied \(em dir_files references files.rowid,
which the merge renumbers \(em and any such artifacts already in the
target are dropped, with an announcement: a merge that changed the files
table has invalidated them. Rerun
.B grouper \-\-prep
on the merged database;
.B grouper \-\-clean\-db
reclaims the space if it matters. The prescan_summary row is neither
copied nor touched: it describes a filesystem walk, which the merge does
not invalidate.
.SH SAFETY CHECKS
All before the target is opened for writing:
.IP \(bu 3
every source must exist and carry the sumtag tables;
.IP \(bu 3
the target may not also be a source, and no source may be given twice
(checked after realpath resolution, so symbolic links cannot disguise
an overlap);
.IP \(bu 3
the same mountpoint path in two sources is refused (see above);
.IP \(bu 3
a merged corpus spanning more than one digest algorithm \(em across the
sources plus whatever target rows survive the merge \(em is refused
without
.BR \-\-allow\-mixed :
digests only compare within one algorithm, so cross-algorithm duplicates
silently fail to match.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-database " TARGET"
The merged database to write. Created if missing (but not under
.BR \-\-dry\-run ).
Existing mountpoints not named by any source are left untouched.
Required.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be merged \(em per-mountpoint
.B would merge
lines with row counts,
.BR "would replace" ,
.B would drop
\(em without writing anything. The target is opened read-only if it
exists and is not created if it does not.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Show one aggregate progress bar on stderr covering all sources:
rows-merged/rows-total with rate, percent, elapsed, and ETA. The
denominator is counted up front (one cheap COUNT per source). Follows
sumtag's conventions: opt-in, appears once the run has taken 2 seconds,
redrawn in place, cleared on completion, suppressed when stderr is not a
terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-mixed
Proceed even when the merged corpus would span more than one digest
algorithm.
.SH OUTPUT
One announcement per mountpoint before its rows stream across
.RB ( "merge " path
.RI ( N " rows from " SOURCE )),
then the house summary block:
.B merge:
(rows, mountpoints, sources \(em the headline prints even at zero),
.B replace:
and
.B drop:
when nonzero, then
.B database:
and
.BR sources: .
Under
.B \-\-dry\-run
every verb gains
.BR would .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Nothing to do: no rows merged, none replaced. In practice only empty
sources, since replacement always rewrites.
.TP
.B 1
The target was (or, under
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
would be) modified \(em the normal successful merge.
.TP
.B 2
Errors, or a safety refusal.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted (Ctrl-C). Commits are per mountpoint, so an interrupted run
keeps the mountpoints it completed; the summary printed reflects only
committed work.
.SH EXAMPLES
Merge three per-volume databases into one analysis database:
.PP
.EX
dbmerge \-\-database merged.sqlite tank1.sqlite tank2.sqlite tank3.sqlite
.EE
.PP
Preview a re-merge of one volume after it was rescanned:
.PP
.EX
dbmerge \-\-database merged.sqlite \-n tank1.sqlite
.EE
.PP
Merge a whole shelf of enclosures with a progress bar:
.PP
.EX
dbmerge \-\-database merged.sqlite \-\-progress tank*.sqlite
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sumtag (1),
.BR grouper (1),
.BR dedupe (1)
